---
// for-cooperatives.astro — evaluation and adoption, for an institution deciding
// whether ICN deserves its attention.
//
// PRIMARY JOB (PUBLIC_SITE_IA.md §3): adoption and evaluation for an
// institution. NOT general explanation — the argument for why institutions need
// this lives on /why-icn, the model on /what-is-icn, and this page links there
// instead of restating them. The previous version re-argued the fragmentation
// problem and the "generic software is not enough" case at length; that is the
// outstanding duplication PUBLIC_SITE_IA.md §3 names.
//
// The page is organised around the four questions an organizer actually has,
// and answers each against what exists today. It never describes a pilot,
// a signup, or a product to join, because none exists.
//
// Claim discipline (ADR-0032): capability statements here are derived from the
// generated project-state projection, with their review date attached, rather
// than asserted in prose. The AdoptionPath figure carries its own readiness
// axis; it is deliberately not the subsystem maturity taxonomy.

import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import AdoptionPath from '../components/AdoptionPath.astro';
import ReadingLadder from '../components/ReadingLadder.astro';
import { PROJECT_STATE as projectState, subsystemsInBand } from '../lib/projectState';

const strongest = subsystemsInBand('strong');
const notYet = subsystemsInBand('notyet');
const total = projectState.subsystems.length;
const reviewed = projectState.newestVerified;
---

<Layout
  title="For cooperatives and institutions"
  activeNav="for-cooperatives"
  description="An honest evaluation page for cooperatives and other democratic institutions: what adopting ICN would ask of you, what you can responsibly try today, what is missing, and how to test the idea without joining a production system that does not exist."
>
  <section class="section coop-top">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <div class="page-hero">
        <span class="badge badge-amber">For cooperatives and institutions</span>
        <h1>Should your institution spend time on this? Here is what we can honestly tell you.</h1>
        <p class="lead">
          There is no product to sign up for and no production network to join.
          ICN is open infrastructure still being built. What exists is a set of
          working parts, a clear account of what is missing, and a way to test
          whether the idea fits your institution before anyone commits anything.
        </p>
        <p class="lead-aside">
          Looking for the case for why institutions need this? That is
          <a href="/why-icn">Why ICN</a>. This page assumes you are already
          asking the practical question.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container narrow prose">
      <h2 id="q-change">What would your cooperative need to change?</h2>
      <p>
        Less than you might fear, and mostly not technical. Nothing in your
        current tools has to be switched off to begin. The first changes are
        about the institution describing itself in writing:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Which groups exist (a working group, a board, a member assembly) and who belongs to each.</li>
        <li>Which rules actually govern spending, admission, and decisions, as opposed to the rules people remember.</li>
        <li>Who is allowed to do what, and what authorizes them.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Many cooperatives find the gap between what the bylaws say and what
        people do is the most useful thing they learn, whether or not they ever
        use ICN. Deliberation and judgement stay with your members. ICN is the
        record and the rules machinery underneath, not a substitute for the
        meeting.
      </p>

      <h2 id="q-today">What can you responsibly try today?</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Follow one invented decision end to end.</strong> The
          <a href="/see-it-work">guided walkthrough</a> uses a fictional
          cooperative and says so at every step. It takes a few minutes and needs no account.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Do the first stage of the adoption path below on paper.</strong>
          Write down where membership, rules, decisions, money, and work live now,
          and who can change each. This needs no ICN software.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Read the evidence.</strong> <a href="/whats-real-now">What's real now</a>
          states, subsystem by subsystem, how far along each part is and what
          kind of evidence backs it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Bring one concrete case.</strong> Open a
          <a href="https://github.com/InterCooperative-Network/icn/discussions" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub Discussion</a>
          describing a real decision your institution struggles to trace. That
          is more useful to the project than general interest, and it commits you to nothing.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="q-missing">What is missing?</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>No production deployment and no live federation between institutions.</li>
        <li>No import tools or connectors for the systems you already run. Moving a function over today means doing the bridging by hand, with the project.</li>
        <li>The experience for ordinary members is the part furthest behind. If you tried to adopt this month, that gap is what you would feel first.</li>
        <li>No announced institutional pilot. Nothing on this site should be read as one.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout callout-caution coop-state">
        <span class="callout-label">The subsystem picture, with its date</span>
        <p>
          At the last per-subsystem review ({reviewed}), {strongest.length} of
          the {total} subsystems were in the strongest maturity band and
          {notYet.length === 0 ? 'none were' : `${notYet.length} ${notYet.length === 1 ? 'was' : 'were'}`} in
          the weakest. That review has not been repeated since, so treat the
          count as a dated snapshot, not a current guarantee. Even the strongest
          band means "exercised by tests or fixtures in this repository", not
          "proven in a working institution".
          <a href="/whats-real-now">See each claim with its evidence</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="q-test">What can you test without joining a production system?</h2>
      <p>
        The part of this that is most worth testing is not our software. It is
        whether <em>describing your institution this way</em> clarifies anything
        for you. You can test that now:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Describe your institution as groups, members, and rules, and see where the description disagrees with practice.</li>
        <li>Trace one past decision from the vote to the action to the record, and note where the trail breaks.</li>
        <li>Tell us where the vocabulary on this site does not match how your members talk. That feedback shapes the work.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If those exercises turn up nothing useful, you have lost an afternoon,
        not a migration.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <h2 id="adoption-path" class="path-heading">If it did fit: a path that never requires a big-bang switch</h2>
      <AdoptionPath />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container narrow prose">
      <h2>What this does not replace</h2>
      <p>
        Deliberation, interpretation, care, negotiation, and responsibility for
        what the institution does in the world are human work, and the hardness
        of that work is not a defect. What infrastructure can improve is the
        fragility of the glue: memory, continuity, accountability, and being
        legible to your own members.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your institution's authority comes from its members and its own rules.
        It does not come from a domain name, a hosting provider, or a login
        service, and nothing described here changes that.
      </p>

      <h2>Ways to engage</h2>
      <p>
        Most institutions should simply observe for now, and that is a fine
        answer. If you want to go further, collaboration with real engineering
        involvement, or contributing your institutional knowledge to the design,
        both start the same way: a Discussion describing your case. The
        maintained routes are on <a href="/get-involved">Get involved</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-ladder">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <ReadingLadder current="for-cooperatives">
        <Fragment slot="extras">
          <a href="/get-involved" class="btn btn-ghost">Get Involved</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/InterCooperative-Network/icn" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn btn-ghost">GitHub ↗</a>
        </Fragment>
      </ReadingLadder>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  /* Clearance for the fixed 64px header; page-specific because the shared
     primitive for this lives on an unmerged branch (#2791). */
  .coop-top {
    padding-top: calc(64px + var(--space-3xl));
  }

  .narrow {
    max-width: 760px;
  }
  .page-hero {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-md);
    align-items: flex-start;
  }
  .page-hero h1 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(1.625rem, 3.6vw, 2.25rem);
    line-height: 1.25;
  }
  .lead {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    color: var(--text-body);
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .lead-aside {
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .prose h2 {
    margin-top: var(--space-2xl);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
  }
  .prose h2:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  .prose p {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-md);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }
  .prose ul {
    margin: var(--space-md) 0;
    padding-left: var(--space-lg);
  }
  .prose li {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .coop-state {
    margin: var(--space-xl) 0;
  }

  .path-heading {
    font-size: var(--text-2xl);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xl);
    max-width: 28ch;
  }
</style>
